  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">More people, especially workers in diverse forms of employment, benefit from innovative, inclusive and equitable employment and labour policies</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">China has made significant progress, creating over 60 million new urban jobs during the 13th Five-Year Plan (2016-2020), expanding social protection, and reducing rural extreme poverty. However, challenges such as a complex macroeconomic environment, rising youth unemployment, an aging population, labour protection in platform economy, and the need for high-quality development persist. 
Country Outcome Programme CHN251 supports China&#x2019;s tripartite constituents in promoting employment policies for decent job creation, improving public employment services (PES), and enhancing worker protections, particularly in the digital and platform economy:

1. Addressing Labour Market Transformations: The ILO supports research on digitalization, demographic changes, climate change, and global economic shifts to provide policy recommendations. Activities include collecting international experiences, organizing policy dialogues on youth employment, and discussing AI&apos;s potential impact on employment.
2. Strengthening PES: The ILO supports modernizing China&#x2019;s PES and labour market information systems through research on digitalization in PES, studies on gig worker protections, and workshops on job openings and labour turnover surveys (JOLTS).
3. Promoting Labour Protection for Platform Workers: With approximately 84 million platform economy workers facing challenges like ambiguous employment relationships and limited protections, the ILO supports legislative advancements, research and policy dialogues on platform work regulation.
4. Enhancing Youth Employment and Entrepreneurship: The ILO collaborates with partners to develop policies and programmes for youth employability, including field studies on innovative models, entrepreneurial training, and adapting training programmes to the evolving job market.</narrative>
  </description>
